                <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">string</span> json = BinkSerializer.Serialize(_canvas.CurrentDocument);
_canvas.LoadDocument(BinkSerializer.Deserialize(json));

<span class="k">string</span> svg = <span class="k">await</span> _canvas.ExportSvgAsync();
<span class="k">byte</span>[] png = <span class="k">await</span> _canvas.ExportPngAsync(<span class="k">new</span> BinkPngExportOptions { Scale = 3f });</pre>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Comparison ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Comparison</p>
        <h2>Why not wrap a JavaScript drawing library?</h2>
        <p class="bb-section-sub">
            A wrapper keeps the drawing in JavaScript and hands .NET a picture of it, or a copy it has to keep in
            step by hand. Bink keeps the drawing in C#, so the document your code reads is the one on the screen.
        </p>
        <div class="bb-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="A JavaScript drawing library wrapper compared with Bink">
            <table class="bb-table bb-compare">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Concern</th><th>JavaScript library wrapper</th><th>Bink</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Where the drawing lives</td><td>In the library's JavaScript objects</td><td>A <code>BinkDocument</code> in C#: layers of immutable elements</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Undo and redo</td><td>The library's own history, out of .NET's reach</td><td>Inverse operations - never snapshots - that your code can group</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Saving</td><td>The library's format, or a picture</td><td>A versioned, compact JSON format, validated against hostile input</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Export</td><td>A screenshot of the canvas</td><td>SVG and PNG through the screen's renderer, and a pure .NET PNG path for servers</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Collaboration</td><td>Bring your own</td><td>Operation sync over any transport, with presence and offline queueing</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Testing</td><td>Needs a browser</td><td>The engine runs in a unit test, a console app or on a server</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

