    @* ============================== FAQ ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Questions</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        <div class="bb-faq">
            <details>
                <summary>Is this a JavaScript drawing library behind interop?</summary>
                <p>
                    No. The stroke model, smoothing, pressure response, hit testing, undo, serialization and export
                    are C#. JavaScript collects pointer and keyboard input and replays a command buffer onto a
                    canvas. The one drawing it does on its own is a provisional trail under Blazor Server, which
                    hides network latency and is replaced by the engine's own geometry.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Does it work with Blazor Server?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes - the same component works in every hosting model. Under Blazor Server the engine runs on the
                    server and a provisional "wet ink" trail is drawn locally while each batch is in flight. A
                    signature field or a form annotation is fine over Server; sustained handwriting over a
                    high-latency connection is what WebAssembly, where the engine runs in the browser, is for.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Does it support pen pressure, tilt and touch?</summary>
                <p>
                    Pressure, tilt and twist are captured when the device reports them, and pressure is simulated
                    from speed when it does not. Palm rejection ignores touch while a pen is down, and with
                    <code>EnableTouchGestures</code> a finger draws and two fingers pan and pinch-zoom.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Can I save a drawing and edit it later?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes - that is the point of a vector document. <code>BinkSerializer</code> writes a versioned,
                    compact JSON format with binary stroke points; <code>TryDeserialize</code> reports a reason
                    instead of throwing on untrusted input, and a document written by a newer version is refused
                    rather than half read.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Is the canvas accessible?</summary>
                <p>
                    The canvas is a labelled, focusable, keyboard-operable element with a live text summary of the
                    drawing, built from the descriptions an application attaches to its elements, and polite
                    announcements of what changed. The strokes themselves cannot be described automatically - the
                    objects around them can.
                </p>
            </details>
        </div>
    </section>

